import Prelude
import Logic
/-!
# Prototype: the dependent eliminator of a Church-encoded `∃` fails

Expected **failure**, the cost of the impredicative route. Run:

    .lake/build/bin/wiz check --no-cache --cache-verify off STC/Prototype/ChurchDep.wiz

Observed:

    ChurchDep.wiz:…: error: type mismatch
      expected: ?m…
      inferred: Q (Ex.intro A P a p)

The encoding quantifies over a constant `R : Prop`, so it cannot be instantiated with a
motive `Q : Ex P → Prop`; and the kernel does not identify `h` with `Ex.intro a p` inside
`Q`, so the step's type does not match. This is the precise sense in which the encoding
is a stopgap for `∨`/`∃`, not a replacement for an inductive proposition whose proofs are
eliminated dependently (the `Derivable`/`LR` derivations of M1).
-/

def Ex.elimDep {A : Type} {P : A → Prop} (h : Ex P) (Q : Ex P → Prop)
    (k : (a : A) → (p : P a) → Q (Ex.intro P a p)) : Q h :=
  h (Q h) (fun a p => k a p)
